1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,400
Good afternoon, everyone!

2
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:08,070
This is the first seminar in preparation for our archaeological fieldwork in Namibia;

3
00:00:08,070 --> 00:00:12,948
we are fantastically lucky to have received partial research funding for this trip from

4
00:00:12,948 --> 00:00:13,731
our Institute,

5
00:00:13,731 --> 00:00:18,950
so I shall expect 200% attention and participation from you all.

6
00:00:18,950 --> 00:00:25,402
First in this seminar, I'm going to give a brief introduction to contemporary research

7
00:00:25,402 --> 00:00:26,384
on rock art,

8
00:00:26,384 --> 00:00:29,931
and in the second part I'm going to give you some do's

9
00:00:29,931 --> 00:00:35,000
and don'ts for our fieldwork trip in April-so please listen very carefully.

10
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:40,220
I'm first going to focus on the interpretation of rock art in Namibia.

11
00:00:40,220 --> 00:00:43,736
We are very fortunate to be going to an area where you can find

12
00:00:43,736 --> 00:00:46,690
some of the most important sites in the entire world.

13
00:00:46,690 --> 00:00:50,341
And I hope to show you how easy it is for everyone to make

14
00:00:50,341 --> 00:00:54,354
mistakes in looking at cultures which are different from our own-the first

15
00:00:54,354 --> 00:00:56,440
and most important lesson we have to learn.

16
00:00:56,440 --> 00:01:02,636
In Namibia there are both paintings and engravings-that's where the surface of the rock

17
00:01:02,636 --> 00:01:03,300
is cut out.

18
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:08,860
Many of the engravings show footprints of animals and most scholars used to think

19
00:01:08,860 --> 00:01:11,830
that the purpose of these was simple and obvious:

20
00:01:11,830 --> 00:01:16,866
this rock art was like a school book with pictures to teach children about

21
00:01:16,866 --> 00:01:17,307
tracks:

22
00:01:17,307 --> 00:01:21,400
which track belonged to which animal-giraffe, lion and so on.

23
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:23,600
But there were some mysteries.

24
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:24,625
First,

25
00:01:24,625 --> 00:01:29,647
when you look at a typical Namibian painting or engraving, you see the tracks

26
00:01:29,647 --> 00:01:32,700
are repeated, there are dozens of tracks for the same animal.

27
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:39,360
You'd expect just one clear illustration if the reason-the aim-was to teach tracking.

28
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:42,320
Now there were two more problems.

29
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:48,185
Why are some of the engravings of animals very accurate as you'd expect-all clearly

30
00:01:48,185 --> 00:01:51,640
identifiable-and others quite unrealistic?

31
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:59,055
And another mystery some of these unrealistic animals that's in the engravings- seem to

32
00:01:59,055 --> 00:02:00,040
be half human.

33
00:02:00,040 --> 00:02:02,730
Some, for example, have got human faces.

34
00:02:02,730 --> 00:02:08,900
Many researchers now think that these were pictures the wise men engraved of themselves.

35
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:13,078
They believed they could use magic to control the animals they had drawn,

36
00:02:13,078 --> 00:02:15,810
so the hunters could then catch them for food.

37
00:02:15,810 --> 00:02:20,447
This shows you some of the dangers of coming from one culture to another,

38
00:02:20,447 --> 00:02:23,820
as we'll be doing, without understanding it fully.

39
00:02:23,820 --> 00:02:29,357
Scholars imagined that children looked at rock art pictures to learn to track-just

40
00:02:29,357 --> 00:02:31,844
because they themselves had learnt skills from pictures;

41
00:02:31,844 --> 00:02:37,140
many researchers now believe that rock art had a much more complex purpose.

42
00:02:37,140 --> 00:02:39,600
And we'll talk more about it next week!

43
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:44,292
Now before I invite you to join in a discussion in this second part

44
00:02:44,292 --> 00:02:49,004
of the seminar, I'd like to make some very important points about our fieldwork-and

45
00:02:49,004 --> 00:02:51,190
in fact any field trip to look at rock art.

46
00:02:51,190 --> 00:02:53,667
We're going to a number of sites,

47
00:02:53,667 --> 00:02:55,620
and we won't always be together.

48
00:02:55,620 --> 00:03:01,743
The single largest problem faced by people who manage the sites is-yes, I'm sure

49
00:03:01,743 --> 00:03:06,360
you've guessed-damage caused by visitors, even though it's usually unintentional.

50
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:11,805
Whenever you do go to a site, don't forget you can learn many things

51
00:03:11,805 --> 00:03:15,040
from observing at a distance instead of walking all over it.

52
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:18,340
This can really help to reduce visitor pressure.

53
00:03:18,340 --> 00:03:23,738
People often say, 'Well, there's only two of us and just this one time',

54
00:03:23,738 --> 00:03:27,110
but maybe thousands of people are saying the same thing.

55
00:03:27,110 --> 00:03:33,274
And then some basic rules to guide you-we'll have our own camp near a

56
00:03:33,274 --> 00:03:33,636
village,

57
00:03:33,636 --> 00:03:37,310
but remember never to camp on a site if you go on your own.

58
00:03:37,310 --> 00:03:40,542
It may be disrespectful to the people of that culture,

59
00:03:40,542 --> 00:03:45,220
and certainly don't make fires, however romantic it may seem.

60
00:03:45,220 --> 00:03:47,972
It's really dangerous in dry areas,

61
00:03:47,972 --> 00:03:52,130
and you can easily burn priceless undiscovered material by doing so.

62
00:03:52,130 --> 00:03:56,560
So, how are we going to enjoy the rock art on our field trip?

63
00:03:56,560 --> 00:04:02,504
By looking at it, drawing it and photographing it-NEVER by touching it

64
00:04:02,504 --> 00:04:03,850
or even tracing it.

65
00:04:03,850 --> 00:04:07,000
Rock art is fragile and precious.

66
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:12,853
Remember that climbing on rocks and in caves can destroy in a moment what

67
00:04:12,853 --> 00:04:14,180
has lasted for centuries.

68
00:04:14,180 --> 00:04:17,220
So no heroics in Namibia, please!

69
00:04:17,220 --> 00:04:20,210
Try to be extra careful and help others to be too.

70
00:04:20,210 --> 00:04:26,828
And lastly please don't even move rocks or branches to take photographs - you

71
00:04:26,828 --> 00:04:30,619
should leave the site intact -I'm sure I can rely on you to do

72
00:04:30,619 --> 00:04:31,000
that.

73
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:32,844
Well,

74
00:04:32,844 --> 00:04:36,071
that's about all I want to say before today's first discussion,

75
00:04:36,071 --> 00:04:39,157
but if you have any questions please ask them now

76
00:04:39,157 --> 00:04:45,230
and don't forget you'll find some fascinating information about world-wide sites on the Internet.

77
00:04:45,230 --> 00:04:47,400
Right, first question then?
